  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nToday - Tuesday: Storm Potential\n\nAfter a foggy start to the day, mostly dry conditions are expected \nuntil early Tuesday morning. There is a low chance (<10%) for a \nshower or storm early this afternoon across the CWA. The better \nstorm chances are in eastern Iowa and northern Illinois for this \nafternoon and evening as a weak boundary shifts northeastward. Some \nof the CAMs are hinting at the possibility of far southwestern \nWisconsin getting in on some of the shower/storm potential (15 to \n20%). Heading into the overnight and early morning timeframe, a well-\ndefined wave forms in eastern South Dakota and western Minnesota. \nAided by 2000 to 3000 J/kg of MLCAPE and DCAPE values of 800 to 1200 \nJ/kg and good amounts of 0-6km shear (40 to 50 kts), a MCS is \nexpected to form in western Minnesota later tonight. This MCS will \nhave the aforementioned parameters to work with, allowing it to \nmaintain itself through the morning. The boundary that this MCS will \nstay on is oriented more west-northwest to east-southeast. This \nmeans if it held together, it would impact northeast Iowa and far \nsouthwest Wisconsin in the early to mid-morning hours Tuesday. CAMs \nare not in good agreement on the exact timing of this system as the \nrange for when it could enter our western counties is between 4AM \nand 8AM. There also remains a little uncertainty on the exact path \nthis MCS takes, as some models have it further north, impacting the \nI-90 corridor. These details will be refined later today as we get \ncloser, however the main hazard with this system will be damaging \nwind gusts. \n\nTuesday Night - Wednesday: Another Potential MCS\n\nMuch of the forecast area should be suppressed from any additional \nstorm development during the afternoon and evening on Tuesday due to \nthe morning MCS, however areas north of I-94 could see some storm \ndevelopment by the evening, as this area is expected to be not be \nimpacted from the morning storms. Heading into the overnight period, \na similar story develops as a wave, that looks more well-defined \nthan tonight's wave, comes down through the Upper Midwest. Where the \nboundary from tonight/Tuesday morning will play an important role \nfor where Tuesday nights/Wednesday mornings MCS goes. Now there is \nuncertainty on if this MCS will survive southeast of a northeast \nNebraska into central Minnesota line, however some models do have a \nMCS or line of storms moving through northern Iowa and southern \nMinnesota Wednesday morning. The GFS is the most northern solution, \nhowever this could be due to its poleward bias. The HRRR has it \nmostly in central and eastern Iowa with a couple northeast Iowa \ncounties potentially involved. Confidence will increase heading into \nTuesday evening once we know where the boundary sets up and how \nconvection is ongoing upstream.\n\nThursday - Early Next Week: More Storm Chances, Slightly Cooler\n\nEnsemble and deterministic guidance both favor a transition from \nzonal/northwest flow to southwest flow aloft by the end of the week \nand a ridge building in for the southeast US this weekend  Due to \nour proximity to the jet stream and not directly under the ridge, \nthe warmer temperatures stay to our south and southeast. Cooler air \nactually moves south into the region keeping temperatures largely in \nthe 70s. With a continued southwesterly fetch of moisture, daily \nstorm chances are possible into early next week.",
